(load "process.carp")

(Project.config "title" "carp-process")
(Project.config "docs-directory" "./docs/")
(Project.config "docs-logo" "")
(Project.config "docs-url" "https://github.com/carpentry-org/carp-process")
(Project.config "docs-styling" "../style.css")
(Project.config "docs-prelude" "Subprocess management for Carp.

Spawns and manages child processes with POSIX semantics: `fork`/`execvp`,
pipe setup, descriptor hygiene and signal-aware waiting. Arguments go to
`execvp(3)` directly, never through a shell, so there is nothing to quote
and nothing to inject.

Everything here is synchronous and reads whole streams into memory.

### Quick start
```
(load \"git@github.com:carpentry-org/carp-process@master\")

(let [out (Process.run-combined
            &(=> (Command.new \"echo\") (Command.arg \"hello\")))]
  (println* (Process.Output.stdout &out)))
```

### Modules
- [`Command`](./Command.html) — building a command to run
- [`Process`](./Process.html) — spawning, waiting, running to completion

### Deadlocks
[`Process.run`](./Process.html#run) drains `stdout` and then `stderr`, one
after the other. A child that fills the `stderr` pipe buffer while `run` is
still reading `stdout` will block, and so will `run`. Use
[`run-combined`](./Process.html#run-combined), which merges the two streams
at the OS level and has only one buffer to drain.")

(save-docs Command Process)
; Carp's save-docs hardcodes the index filename as <title>_index.html.
; Rename to docs/index.html.
(defn main []
  (System.system "mv ./docs/carp-process_index.html ./docs/index.html"))
